First, we note that Marissa has already hiked 4 miles in 1 hour and then 2 miles in another hour. This means she has covered 6 miles in 2 hours.

To achieve an average speed of 4 miles per hour over the entire 12-mile trail, we calculate the total time she has to complete the hike:
$$
\text{Total time} = \frac{\text{Total distance}}{\text{Average speed}} = \frac{12 \text{ miles}}{4 \text{ mph}} = 3 \text{ hours}
$$

Marissa has already spent 2 hours on the first 6 miles. Therefore, the remaining time she has to cover the last 6 miles is:
$$
3 \text{ hours} - 2 \text{ hours} = 1 \text{ hour}
$$

To find the required speed for the remaining 6 miles in 1 hour:
$$
\text{Required speed} = \frac{\text{Remaining distance}}{\text{Remaining time}} = \frac{6 \text{ miles}}{1 \text{ hour}} = 6 \text{ mph}
$$

Thus, the speed Marissa needs to walk the remaining distance is:
$$
